# Line endings, and why this file exists.
#
# THE DEFECT IT CLOSES. `core.autocrlf` is true on the maintainer's machine, so
# every `git checkout` rewrote the working tree with CRLF. Thirty-plus tests in
# this suite read their own source with `readFileSync(..., "utf8")` and then
# match it with regexes anchored on `\n` — `/\\\n\s*/` to fold a shell
# continuation, `^`/`$` in multiline mode, `split("\n")`. None of those match
# `\r\n`, so those guards silently stop reading the file they guard.
#
# It cost a real incident on 2026-10-05: `tests/ops/backup-workflow.test.ts`
# folds the `pg_dump` invocation across its backslash continuations to assert
# `--schema=public` is passed. With CRLF the fold never happened, the helper
# returned `"$PG_DUMP" "$PGDUMP_URL" \` with no flags, and the test reported
# "the pg_dump call passes no --schema filter" about a file that carries it on
# the very next line. The full suite had been green an hour earlier, on the
# same commit, because the working tree still held LF from before a checkout.
#
# THAT IS THE WORST SHAPE A TEST CAN HAVE HERE: it passes on Linux CI and fails
# on a Windows checkout of the identical commit, so the result depends on how
# the file happened to be written rather than on what it says. The same class
# took out `tests/app/finance/uncapped-totals.test.ts` at the same moment.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises every text file to LF IN THE WORKING TREE, not
# just in the index, which is what the readers actually see. Binary files are
# detected by `text=auto` and left alone. There are no .bat/.cmd/.ps1 files in
# this repo, which are the usual reason to exempt something from this rule; if
# one is ever added it needs an `eol=crlf` line of its own or Windows will not
# run it.
#
# The tests were ALSO made line-ending-tolerant rather than relying on this
# file alone, because a zip download or a clone with different settings is not
# obliged to honour it. Two defences, deliberately — see
# tests/lib/harness/source-scan.ts, which is tested against CRLF input
# directly so that its guard cannot pass on the strength of this file.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Lockfiles are text and benefit from the same normalisation; called out so
# nobody "fixes" a noisy diff by marking them binary.
package-lock.json text eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the assets `text=auto` should already detect. An image
# that ever gets CRLF-mangled is corrupt, and the corruption is invisible until
# something tries to decode it.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
